On Wed, Jul 22, 2015 at 12:12 AM, guskant <gusni.kantu@gmail.com> wrote:

>
> Relative clauses should care about the main bridi, because the bridi in
> relative clauses share their universe of discourse with the main bridi.
> About this example of fragment, I said only "logically meaningless", but it
> does not bring any problem by itself.
>
> The problem occurs in the case that plural relative clauses appears with
> their own prenex, sometimes nested in logical connectives. Which prenex is
> the outmost? How can the negations, numbers and logical connectives are
> transformed into a prenex normal form?
>

I don't understand what you mean. Could you give an example where you find
a prenex in a relative clause problematic? In general, a prenex in a
non-restrictive relative clause cannot be exported to the main bridi prenex:

   ro da zo'u da prami la alis noi no de zo'u ke'a prami de
   = ro da zo'u da prami la alis .i ta'o no de zo'u la alis prami de
   =/= ro da no de zo'u da prami la alis .i je la alis prami de

mu'o mi'e xorxes

